  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates an electrochemical system for capturing and releasing carbon dioxide using stacked electrodes. On the left, several parallel plate-like electrodes are arranged with cathodes and an anode, and large arrows show CO₂ moving into the device. A highlighted circular region suggests that the active chemistry occurs within the electrode material.\n\nThe right side zooms in on the proposed molecular mechanism. It shows a quinone-like aromatic unit in a polymer or repeated structure, marked by “n,” that can switch between two states under electron transfer. In the “adsorption mode,” the reduced form reacts with carbon dioxide, indicated by “2CO₂ + 2e⁻,” to form carbonate-like adducts attached to oxygen sites on the molecule. In the “desorption mode,” the reverse electrochemical step releases CO₂ and regenerates the original quinone structure.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a reversible, redox-driven CO₂ separation concept: applying electrons converts the material into a CO₂-binding state, while reversing the process liberates the gas. The central comparison is between the unbound molecular form and the CO₂-bound form, linked to electrode operation in the cell.",
